We faced the Karokhan.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.620
I've learned a piece.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.880
I've learned a piece and

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.520
Then we managed to recover and win bonus.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.980
Okay, we're wide against decode.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:23.860
I

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.720
Mean again, we've been toggling between

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.060
jabal London and 1e4, but let's switch

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:28.980
back

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.740
To our main repertoire and go for e4

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.940
especially because we're facing the

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.280
center counter also known as the

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:35.980
Scandinavian

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.260
Which I believe is the first time that

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.060
we have faced it in the speedrun.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.580
Okay, Knight f6

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.320
This is less popular than Queen takes

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.100
d5, which is the sort of more in the

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:47.800
spirit of the Scandi

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.640
I don't know if Knight f6 has a name.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.680
Apparently it's called the modern variation.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.160
There is something called the

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.420
Portuguese gambit I want to say which

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:59.140
goes d4 Bishop g4, but many years ago

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.520
Many many many years ago around 2005

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.580
2006 one of my first coaches taught me

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.360
a line

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.580
That essentially avoids a lot of the

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:09.400
theory of the Portuguese gambit on some

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.480
of the trickier lines

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.100
And it gives white not as big of an

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.900
advantage as you would get if you play

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.340
the absolute principled stuff

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.380
But what I like is that it just gives

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.300
you an easy-to-play position and it

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.220
avoids a lot of the theory

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:22.620
So the line goes like this and I've

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.680
done this before on a previous speed

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:24.780
run

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.680
I remember that we faced this you

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.660
deliver a check on b5 which should make

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:30.920
should make a lot of sense to you

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.760
It's a developing move and you're

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.780
forcing a piece to appear on d7, which

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.640
by definition

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.160
is severing the connection between the

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.420
black Queen and the pawn on d5 and

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.740
Fundamentally, there are two approaches

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.700
to playing this position.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.160
You could try to cling to the d5 pawn

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.860
So what you could try to do here is

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.500
play Bishop takes d7 Queen takes d7 and

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.200
c4 and a lot of less

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.220
Experienced players would gravitate

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.320
toward that but I'll show you a couple

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.780
of analogs after the game this approach

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.620
in many openings

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:01.580
This is not good because after black

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.260
plays c6 he attains massive

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:05.240
compensation

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.960
Positionally for the pawn in the form

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.160
of dominance over key central squares

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.900
and it's just not an easy position to

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:12.120
play

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.320
So the move that my coach taught me is

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.820
to drop the bishop actually back to e2

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:18.920
The point is you give away the d5 pawn

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.360
and now you occupy the center with d4

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.560
and you get a slight advantage

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.820
In an easy-to-play position.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:25.380
What was the point of this whole

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.580
operation with bishop b5 Bishop b2?

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.240
Well, it was just a force black's

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:30.940
bishop to appear on d7.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:31.900
You might say well

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.620
Why are we forcing isn't it good for

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.660
black to develop a piece?

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.160
Well, not quite because the bishop on

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.300
d7 is a very awkward piece.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:38.960
It's again

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.960
It's cutting the connection between the

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:41.940
black Queen in the center.

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.780
It's preventing the Knight from

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:44.620
accessing that square

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.520
It's just not developed on a good

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:46.720
square

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.720
So that is why in this line you often

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.240
see experienced Scandi players actually

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.940
reposition the bishop on f5 c5

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.040
I've never seen before it's a very

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:56.860
sensible move

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.260
My guess is that if we take on c5 black

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.020
wants to give Queen a5 check and then

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.820
pick up the pawn and really

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.320
Contest our control over the center.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.920
So there are several ways that we can

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:06.660
fight this move

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.420
We can ignore it and just keep

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:09.540
developing our pieces

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.980
But if possible, I don't just want to

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.660
give away our d4 pawn without a fight

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.880
now in the allopin cecilian

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:18.280
There is a similar type of idea

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:21.440
that involves pushing c4 and then

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.120
driving the pawn into d5 and

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.880
establishing a more

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:28.780
A firmer presence in the center, which

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.140
I think makes a lot of sense here

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.760
I think we should go c4 and counter

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.780
attack black's knight

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:35.380
Our opponent is still playing instantly

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.420
Which tells me that he proud this is

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:39.260
probably some sort of obscure theory

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.500
Now here again d takes c5 is not stupid

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:46.440
Because what we could say is okay,

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:48.400
we're not trying to win a pawn

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.920
We're trying to gain a bunch of tempi

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:51.680
for development

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.340
After queen a5 check there's one tempo

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:56.520
and then when black takes on c5

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.080
There's another tempo we could play

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.380
bishop e3 and then essentially complete

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.000
our minor piece development with knight

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:02.460
f3

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.980
So that I think is a great approach

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.300
as well

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.580
So I like the look of d takes c5 but d5

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.520
is what we initially plan to do

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.220
I think we should stick to our guns

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.100
here and play d5

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:17.020
e6, okay

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.360
So here the instinct of most people I

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.380
think is to play knight c3

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:24.520
And then to meet ed with cd

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.920
but the problem is that when

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.760
That pawn chain turns into an isolated

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.920
pawn our strategy loses a lot of its

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:32.580
luster

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.920
Because the d5 pawn becomes more of a

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.100
liability than a strength

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.420
so I think

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.480
A more prudent approach here is

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.620
actually to take on e6 which I know is

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.360
A little bit anticlimactic because we

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.400
did all of this and now we're just

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.180
giving away our center

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.200
But if you look concretely it should

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:50.520
make some more sense de if black plays

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.480
fe well, obviously that ruins black

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:52.940
structure

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.100
We're happy if black plays bishop takes

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:55.620
e6

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.660
Then we're able to trade queens and

00:04:57.660 --> 00:04:59.520
force black's king onto a very awkward

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.620
square on d8

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.120
Which we could use as fuel for our

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.360
subsequent development and you should

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.960
be familiar with this general concept

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:07.600
where essentially you're able to castle

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:08.480
queen side

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.160
With check i'm sure you've probably

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.580
done this in other openings and I think

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:13.360
that white has

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:16.240
A very small but a very stable edge in

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.980
the end game and we haven't had that

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:18.920
many end games in general

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.140
So I wanted an excuse to play an end

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:21.240
game

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.720
Okay, clearly this tells me that our

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:23.900
opponent is not in preparation

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.180
This is just complete bluff and he's

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:27.380
playing this like a bullet game because

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:28.460
fe is a very bad move.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.280
I think

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.760
Now black is saddled with permanent

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.060
weakness of the e6 pawn

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.400
And of course when black castles king

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.780
side the king side is not going to be

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.000
quite as safe of a place

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.880
As it used to be but let's speed up a

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:40.160
little bit.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.280
I don't want to lose on time

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:42.520
So now

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:44.620
The next stage is for us to just

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.680
develop our pieces really in any order

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.220
I don't think it particularly matters

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.320
in what order we develop our pieces

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:52.700
We do want to protect the d4 square so

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.960
that black doesn't quickly establish a

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.500
knight on d4

00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:57.860
But we just do that by playing knight f3.

00:05:58.080 --> 00:05:58.500
Let's start.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.840
Let's start with knight c3.

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.020
It doesn't matter

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.880
Now we'll play knight f3 gain a little

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:04.580
bit of time and now we'll castle we can

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:06.740
just basically play automatically here

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.340
these next couple of moves

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.800
But then we should take a moment to

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.700
think because this bishop we can

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.500
develop it to many different spots and

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:15.820
this is an important decision

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.240
Where we develop our bishop?

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:20.980
I see at least four options actually

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.340
bishop b3 bishop f4 bishop g5

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.940
And let's not forget about the queen

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:26.660
side fianchetto.

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:28.680
We can play b3 and bishop b2

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:31.360
Now let's take the opposite view.

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.080
What is black likely to do here?

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:35.480
Well one annoying idea is for black to

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:37.900
try to utilize this pawn effectively to

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.340
push it up to e5

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.780
And to establish an outpost for the

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:44.240
knight on d4 that is a positional

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:45.740
Weak, well not a weakness, but that is

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:46.500
a risk

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.440
That we need to we need to fight.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.160
I I don't I don't think we should allow

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:52.940
black to establish an outpost on d4

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.000
Because if we trade on d4 then the pawn

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:56.620
is replaced

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:58.220
The knight is replaced by a very

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:00.900
annoying pawn much like our pawn on d5

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.780
and we won't have the machinery

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.720
Available to contest that pawn

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.180
So for that reason I like bishop f4

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.580
because it's prophylactic

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.140
Now you might look at this move and say

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:10.560
well

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.000
I don't like the fact that we're

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.900
stepping into the x-ray of the rook

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.520
But an x-ray in and of itself isn't all

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:16.780
that bad.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.440
We have to check to see if black has

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:18.980
discoveries

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.720
Unfortunately, I think there is what I

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.800
would call a positional discovery

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.720
Which is a discovered attack that is

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.200
not intended to win a piece.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.860
It is intended to engineer a favorable

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:30.320
trade

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.740
By which I mean black has this weird

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.040
move knight f6 to e4

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.380
And after we take on e4 black is able

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:39.180
to eliminate the dark's court bishop

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:41.160
And make it easier for himself to

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.300
secure the knight on d4

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:45.140
So bishop f4 has a bit of a I don't

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:46.540
know whether to call it a tactical or

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.240
positional drawback

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.840
Let me keep thinking for a minute for a

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:49.200
moment

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.720
So if we're not playing bishop f4

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.640
We might want to resort to b3.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.860
We also might want to play bishop to e3

00:07:56.860 --> 00:07:58.380
Even though it looks a little bit more

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:00.800
passive bishop to g5 is also possible

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:01.620
here

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.060
From the perspective of like safest

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:05.140
developing square.

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:06.820
I think let's play bishop g5.

00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:09.200
Let's just play the sort of most

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.220
Straightforward textbook developing

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:13.960
move and if our opponent plays e5 we'll

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.100
cross that bridge when we get there

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.680
Okay, obviously bishop h4 is the

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:20.330
typical retreating move

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.150
Knight h5 which is I I kind of expected

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:24.030
this actually

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.430
Now let's evaluate this positionally

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.690
well positionally it leaves black after

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:30.090
the trade of dark squared bishops

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:32.170
Which by the way, we are not forced to

00:08:32.170 --> 00:08:33.970
engage and we can drop the bishop back

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.090
to g3 people often forget about this

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:36.570
prospect

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:39.010
But the thing is then we give black the

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:41.750
bishop pair and this bishop from e7 can

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.490
reposition itself to f6

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:45.830
And again, we're fighting for control

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:46.770
of the d4 square.

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.150
That is the sort of

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.570
Unstated battle in this position.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:52.390
So from that perspective we should take

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:53.010
on e7

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.850
And here we need to think what does

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.410
black want?

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:58.050
Well black wants at least two things

00:08:58.050 --> 00:09:00.110
There is this old idea e5 knight d4

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:03.350
It's less scary now because e5 can be

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:04.870
met with knight to d5 and we get an

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.230
outpost of our own

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:09.590
The scarier prospect is knight to f4

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.570
So you might come up with the idea of

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.210
g3, but g3 weakens our king side a

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:14.950
little bit

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:16.410
So I wouldn't want to make this move

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.250
unless we were sort of desperate.

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.650
Well, not desperate and and really it's

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:21.750
not that weakening

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.090
It's really not that weakening because

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:25.710
the dark the light squared bishop for

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:28.010
black is not able to access

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.990
key squares g4 and h3

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:32.570
Furthermore after g3

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.690
We create this potential idea of knight

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:37.370
to h4 when the knight has access to the

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.010
forkable square on g6

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.110
g3 I like the look of a little bit more

